WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Question 0057: ants on a rod,
a junior-level question.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:10.100
Four parts: when it all ends, how many collisions,
when a given ant falls, and a ring.

3
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:15.367
Ants stand on a rod 100 cm long,
each at a different point.

4
00:00:15.367 --> 00:00:19.367
Each ant faces left or right
and walks one centimetre a second.

5
00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:24.467
When two ants meet, both turn around
at once. An ant that reaches an end falls.

6
00:00:24.467 --> 00:00:28.867
The page's example: six ants,
at 10, 25, 40, 60, 75 and 90.

7
00:00:29.667 --> 00:00:34.867
Part A: 100 ants on the rod, in any order.
What is the longest time until all have fallen?

8
00:00:35.567 --> 00:00:40.767
Two ants, A and B, walk toward each other.
On top they turn when they meet, as in the question.

9
00:00:40.767 --> 00:00:45.367
Below, the same two, but here they pass
through each other, like ghosts.

10
00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:49.367
We run both together.
They meet in the middle, at 50.

11
00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:53.833
After the meeting: on top A goes back left,
below A carries on to the right.

12
00:00:53.833 --> 00:00:57.833
But the places are exactly the same: one point left
and one point right. Only the colours swapped.

13
00:00:57.833 --> 00:01:01.833
Without the names you cannot tell them apart.
That is the whole trick: think of ghosts.

14
00:01:02.633 --> 00:01:08.033
Now the page's six ants. Under the rod
each ant draws a line: place against time.

15
00:01:08.033 --> 00:01:13.367
Time runs downward. At every meeting
the ants' lines break.

16
00:01:13.367 --> 00:01:18.933
We go on until the rod is empty:
the last ant falls after 90 seconds.

17
00:01:19.233 --> 00:01:23.533
And now the same run with ghosts,
which walk straight and never turn.

18
00:01:23.533 --> 00:01:29.267
Each ghost is a straight line. These are exactly
the same lines, only in other colours.

19
00:01:30.067 --> 00:01:35.567
A ghost walking left from p leaves after p seconds,
one walking right after 100 minus p.

20
00:01:35.567 --> 00:01:40.167
In the example the six ghosts' times
are 90, 25, 60, 60, 75 and 10.

21
00:01:40.167 --> 00:01:44.633
The rod is empty when the last ghost leaves:
the maximum, 90 seconds.

22
00:01:44.633 --> 00:01:50.100
Every time is at most 100, and 100 happens
when an ant stands at an end facing inward.

23
00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:54.100
And it does not depend on how many ants:
with one or with 100, the answer is 100 seconds.

24
00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.900
Now let's write it in Python.

25
00:02:02.433 --> 00:02:06.933
last_fall takes the places and the directions:
+1 right, -1 left.

26
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:16.000
For each ant, the time of its ghost:
p if it walks left, else 100 minus p.

27
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.000
And we return the maximum. There is
no simulation of collisions at all.

28
00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:24.200
Let's run it on the page's six ants.

29
00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:28.000
The times are 90, 25, 60, 60, 75 and 10,
and the maximum is 90.

30
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.767
The page's box draws the lines too:
place across, and time going down.

31
00:02:33.767 --> 00:02:38.267
After 25 seconds four collisions
have happened, and two ants have fallen.

32
00:02:38.267 --> 00:02:42.767
Almost at the end, at 89.5 seconds,
only ant 4 is left. The lines break at each meeting.

33
00:02:42.767 --> 00:02:47.267
The "ghosts" switch turns them into straight lines:
the very same lines, only the colours swap.

34
00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:52.533
The cost: one pass over the ants,
O(n) time and O(1) memory.

35
00:02:53.333 --> 00:02:58.800
Trap: simulating collision after collision.
With 100 ants there can be 2500 of them.

36
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:04.200
The ghosts spare all of that: the time depends
only on each ant's own place and direction.

37
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:10.200
Part B: how many collisions in a given arrangement?
And what is the most with 100 ants?

38
00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:16.300
Every collision is a point where two lines
cross. Let's look at the ghosts' straight lines.

39
00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:20.700
The example has five crossings,
so five collisions of ants.

40
00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:24.700
Two straight lines cross only
if they head toward each other.

41
00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:30.800
A pair meets if the left one goes right
and the right one goes left. Other pairs never meet.

42
00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:35.700
And they always meet in time, halfway,
before either of them leaves the rod.

43
00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:41.666
We count: for each ant going left,
how many going right are on its left.

44
00:03:41.666 --> 00:03:46.066
25 meets 10. 60 and 75
each meet 10 and 40.

45
00:03:46.066 --> 00:03:49.566
In all 1 plus 2 plus 2:
five collisions.

46
00:03:50.366 --> 00:03:55.333
With k ants going right,
there are at most k times n minus k pairs.

47
00:03:55.333 --> 00:03:59.733
And the most is when all those going right
are left of all those going left.

48
00:03:59.733 --> 00:04:04.833
k(n − k) is largest at k = n/2:
with 100 ants, 50 times 50, 2500.

49
00:04:05.633 --> 00:04:08.633
Now let's write it in Python.

50
00:04:12.133 --> 00:04:16.633
collisions takes the places and the directions
and returns the number of collisions.

51
00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:22.700
Two counters: count for the collisions,
and right for the right-goers passed so far.

52
00:04:25.266 --> 00:04:29.433
We go over the ants from left to right,
after sorting them by place.

53
00:04:31.433 --> 00:04:35.933
An ant going right: add one
to right. It will meet those on its right.

54
00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:42.400
An ant going left meets every right-goer
on its left: add right.

55
00:04:43.566 --> 00:04:46.066
At the end we return count.

56
00:04:47.366 --> 00:04:51.466
Let's run it on the page's six ants.
Both counters start at 0.

57
00:04:51.466 --> 00:04:55.866
10 goes right: right = 1.

58
00:04:55.866 --> 00:05:00.266
25 goes left and meets 10:
count = 1.

59
00:05:00.266 --> 00:05:04.666
40 goes right: right = 2.

60
00:05:04.666 --> 00:05:09.066
60 goes left and meets 10 and 40:
count = 3.

61
00:05:09.066 --> 00:05:13.466
75 also goes left and meets both:
count = 5.

62
00:05:13.466 --> 00:05:18.366
90 goes right, and there is no one on its right
to meet it: right = 3.

63
00:05:18.366 --> 00:05:21.966
The loop is over: the function returns 5.

64
00:05:22.766 --> 00:05:28.266
The cost: the sort is O(n log n),
the pass itself O(n). O(n) memory for the list.

65
00:05:29.066 --> 00:05:34.066
Trap: counting every pair with opposite
directions. Here 25 goes left and 40 right.

66
00:05:34.066 --> 00:05:39.533
They move apart and never meet.
Count only pairs that face each other.

67
00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:45.530
Part C: when, and from which side, does
the k-th ant from the left fall? Which falls last?

68
00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:51.430
The first idea: ants never pass each other.
They only meet and turn.

69
00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:56.230
So the order along the rod never changes:
ant 1 is always the leftmost.

70
00:05:56.230 --> 00:06:00.230
The ant that falls on the left is always
the leftmost one still on the rod.

71
00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:06.530
The second idea: every ghost leaving on the left
drops an ant on the left. Three ghosts go left.

72
00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:10.530
So ants 1, 2 and 3 fall on the left,
after 25, 60 and 75 seconds, in order.

73
00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:14.763
The ghosts going right leave
after 90, 60 and 10 seconds.

74
00:06:14.763 --> 00:06:18.763
On the right the rightmost ant falls first:
ant 6 at 10, ant 5 at 60, ant 4 at 90.

75
00:06:19.563 --> 00:06:24.163
The table: for each ant, which side
it falls from, and when.

76
00:06:24.163 --> 00:06:29.463
Ant 4 started at 60 walking left,
yet it falls on the right, last, at 90.

77
00:06:30.263 --> 00:06:33.263
Now let's write it in Python.

78
00:06:38.396 --> 00:06:42.896
fall_times returns when each ant falls,
in their order from the left.

79
00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:51.030
The times of the ghosts leaving on the left:
p of the left-goers, smallest first.

80
00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:59.730
The times of the ghosts leaving on the right:
100 minus p of the right-goers.

81
00:07:01.763 --> 00:07:06.263
The left ones first, then the right ones
reversed: the rightmost ant falls first.

82
00:07:07.363 --> 00:07:11.663
On the example: on the left leave
the ghosts of 25, 60 and 75.

83
00:07:11.663 --> 00:07:16.063
On the right: 100 minus 10, 40 and 90,
sorted: 10, 60 and 90.

84
00:07:16.063 --> 00:07:21.163
The result: 25, 60, 75, then 90, 60 and 10.
The maximum is ant 4's, 90.

85
00:07:21.963 --> 00:07:25.930
In the page's box: the rod, and under it
when each ant falls.

86
00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:29.530
After 10 seconds ant 6 falls on the right,
the first one.

87
00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:32.663
After 25 seconds ant 1 falls on the left.

88
00:07:32.663 --> 00:07:37.630
After 60 seconds two fall together:
ant 2 on the left and ant 5 on the right.

89
00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:42.763
At 75 ant 3 falls on the left. Only
ant 4 is left; it will fall on the right at 90.

90
00:07:43.563 --> 00:07:48.663
The cost: two sorts, O(n log n),
and two lists of times, O(n).

91
00:07:49.463 --> 00:07:54.463
Trap: "an ant falls on the side
it faces". Ant 4 faces left.

92
00:07:54.463 --> 00:07:58.863
But it falls on the right. What walks straight
is the ghost, not the ant.

93
00:07:59.663 --> 00:08:04.763
Part D, briefly: the rod bends into a ring,
with no ends, and no ant falls.

94
00:08:05.463 --> 00:08:10.630
The same six ants, but four walk
clockwise, and only 60 and 75 against it.

95
00:08:10.630 --> 00:08:15.796
Each ghost completes a lap in 100 seconds:
the set of places and directions returns.

96
00:08:15.796 --> 00:08:20.763
But the circular order turns: each ant
moves s = R − L places, here 2.

97
00:08:20.763 --> 00:08:25.363
After 100 seconds: ant 0 stands
where ant 2 started, and so for all.

98
00:08:25.363 --> 00:08:28.563
After 200 seconds: two more places.

99
00:08:28.563 --> 00:08:32.730
After 300 seconds all are home,
in the same place and the same direction.

100
00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:38.230
In general: n over gcd(n, s) laps.
Here 6 over 2, three laps: 300 seconds.

101
00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:45.730
To sum up: ghosts that pass through each other
solve all four parts.

102
00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:50.630
Try your own arrangements in the box
on the page of question 0057 on the site.
